Seasonal allergic rhinitis presenting with episodic sneezing and clear rhinorrhea
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Paroxysmal sneezing, Clear watery rhinorrhea, Pale boggy turbinates

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-034
Reason for Visit: Severe, abrupt onset of facial congestion and violent sneezing requiring assessment to rule out acute infectious respiratory disease or asthma exacerbation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted sudden profuse rhinorrhea, sneezing paroxysms, and conjunctival injection. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary prior to the next in-hours period to auscultate the lungs, rule out an evolving acute viral respiratory infection, and ensure airway patency was not compromised by severe mucosal edema.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I can't stop sneezing, my nose is running like a tap, and my eyes are incredibly itchy.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes paroxysmal bouts of violent sneezing and profuse clear watery rhinorrhea, suddenly worsening over the past 24 hours. Reports pruritus of the nasal mucosa and palate. Notes bilateral conjunctival hyperemia and epiphora. Describes an exacerbation of symptoms associated with the current high pollen season. Mentions a transverse nasal crease from repetitive upward rubbing. Complete absence of purulent nasal discharge, high fever, throat pain, or productive cough.
Functional Status: Fully independent, but socially impaired by continuous sneezing.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme discomfort and sleep disruption.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/76 | HR: 82 | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, frequently wiping nose, watery eyes, sneezing during interview.
Physical Exam: Anterior rhinoscopy reveals bilateral pale and boggy nasal turbinates. Diagnostic triad confirmed: paroxysmal sneezing, clear watery rhinorrhea, and pale boggy turbinates. Infraorbital venous pooling presenting as allergic shiners is visible. Oropharynx is clear without exudate. Normal pulmonary auscultation without wheeze or prolonged expiratory phase. Absence of cervical lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Windows are open, visible pollen dusting on outdoor furniture.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking no regular preventative allergy medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Seasonal allergic rhinitis presenting with episodic sneezing and clear rhinorrhea - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Viral Upper Respiratory Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Sudden paroxysmal onset without a viral prodrome, absence of fever, and intense pruritus of the palate and eyes.

Asthma Exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear lung fields on auscultation, absence of wheeze, and normal oxygen saturations.

Acute Bacterial Sinusitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear watery discharge rather than purulent thick mucus, and absence of severe unilateral facial pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with explosive upper respiratory symptoms mimicking a severe acute viral infection. An urgent face-to-face assessment was necessary to physically evaluate the upper and lower respiratory tracts, effectively ruling out early lower airway bronchospasm (asthma) or an infectious etiology requiring different public health management. The clinical examination confidently isolated the severe reaction to an IgE-mediated environmental allergy, allowing for immediate targeted antihistamine therapy and avoiding unnecessary antibiotic use.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescription for a second-generation oral antihistamine (e.g., fexofenadine) for immediate relief. Recommendation to start an intranasal corticosteroid spray (e.g., mometasone) for long-term mucosal control. Saline eye drops for conjunctival flush.
Positive Behavioral Change: Keep bedroom windows strictly closed during peak pollen season and run an air purifier, anchoring the habit by placing the purifier switch next to the bedside lamp.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): HEPA air purifier.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 4 weeks to assess the efficacy of the intranasal steroid.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you suddenly develop difficulty breathing, wheezing, swelling of the lips or tongue, or a sensation that your throat is closing.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical allergic rhinitis; identifying exact environmental triggers may require formal allergy testing.
Clinical Course: Oral antihistamines will significantly reduce sneezing and itching within 1 hour. Nasal steroids require several days to reduce the boggy congestion.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if your nasal discharge becomes thick, yellow, or foul-smelling, or if you develop a high fever and facial pain.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A huge exposure to pollen triggered mast cells in your nose and eyes to release massive amounts of histamine, causing the sudden swelling and fluid release. Antihistamine pills block this chemical immediately, while the nasal spray reduces the chronic swelling over time.
Actionable Steps: Take the antihistamine daily. Use the nasal spray consistently. Shower before bed to wash pollen out of your hair so you don't transfer it to your pillow.

